
Password file

Each user of Unix requires a "userid". The table of userids is the file
/etc/passwd. Users can all use the same userid (even at the same time!)
but it is usually preferable for each user to have his own entry in the
/etc/passwd file.

It is very easy to add entries to the passwd file. Simply use the system
editor (ed, vi, etc.) and add a line for each userid you want.

The format of the line is:
	
	There are 7 fields, which are colon delimited. The fields are -

		userid - put the userid at the beginning of the line,
			and then a colon.
		password - LEAVE THIS EMPTY FOR NOW -- thus put another
			colon.
		numeric user id -  this is a unique number for
			this userid.
		numeric group id - this is a number  which is shared amongst
			the members of a "group". Group membership
			influences read-write-execute permissions to files.
			(See the permissions file in /usr/unix.info)
			The file /etc/group has the "mapping" between
			the numeric group number and the name of the group
		user's name - the name of the user
		user's home directory - the directory that the user is in
			immediately after logging in. It is also the
			directory that the user is sent to if he issues
			the "cd" command with no arguments.
		shell - the name of the program to use as the user's shell.
			This is typically /bin/sh or /bin/csh

Here is an example of an entry for a user named Joe Jones. We will give him
the user number 123, and assign him to the  "users" group - 100 . (Look
at /etc/group to confirm this.) We will assign /usr/joe as his
home directory, and Joe will use the regular shell.

joe::123:100:Joe Jones:/usr/joe:/bin/sh

There are 2 more steps to establish a new userid:

1	You MUST make the directory that you have indicated as the
	user's home directory. In the previous example this would be
		mkdir /usr/joe

2	You MAY optionally assign a passwd to the userid. (Or the user
	may do it himself.) To assign a password you must login as the
	user and then run the "passwd" program. You will be prompted
	for the password, which must be 6 or more characters in length.


